{"step_index":61,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-29T02:41:27Z","input_tokens":2998,"cache_read_tokens":54247,"output_tokens":3601,"thinking":"Initial project concept centered on a Sci-Fi Supercar or F1 2035 theme, starting with a Blender file (`scifi_f1_2035.blend`) based on sci-fi references. Early development involved GT supercar prototypes, such as `supercar_gt_v2` and `supercar_gt_v3`.\n\n\nAddressing previous version deficiencies, a complete rebuild emphasizes realistic F1 car features. Implementing parametric wheels with accurate dimensions and materials (tires, rims, central locknuts) improves visual fidelity. Furthermore, a new braking system incorporating carbon-ceramic discs and air ducts enhances the simulation's realism.\nThe aerodynamics received a significant overhaul, incorporating a functional DRS, narrowed endplates, and a Venturi undertray. Conformal decals replace floating cards, enhancing the model's visual integration. A procedural carbon fiber material and heat-tinted titanium exhaust add further realism.\nA sophisticated, hierarchical rigging system ensures accurate animation. The aim is a concise hierarchy that facilitates realistic motion, unlike the previous iteration's problematic drift angles.\n\n\nRigging now accounts for 236 parts, rigidly bound to corresponding bones; expression drivers enable real-time viewport control of key vehicle dynamics. The track is a 1779-meter procedural circuit with realistic geometry, now fixed from the self-intersecting loop.\n\nVehicle dynamics have been refined for authenticity, limiting the maximum slip angle and incorporating realistic pitch, roll, and suspension behavior. Soundtrack integration with *After Hours* will be achieved via music beat-cut techniques and multi-camera cinematography.\n\n\nA sequence of 10 camera shots, precisely synchronized across 720 frames, is the current focus, showcasing the F1 car's dynamics. The scene includes a livery update to Mercedes-AMG PETRONAS bare-carbon black with Petronas teal accents and associated sponsor branding.\n\nThe deliverable is a 30-second, 1080p, 24fps video file (`renders_v4/f1_merc_petronas_30s_beatcut.mp4`) with beat-cut editing and a complete music mix. A master project file (`scifi_f1_2035_v4_racing.blend`) contains the complete model, rig, animation, and 10 camera cuts.\n\n\nThe goal is to create a slimmed-down, standalone project folder, the core of which requires the final Blender project file.  This must include all essential textures for the car's materials, ensuring the project renders correctly out of the box.  The folder will also house the final video render and high-resolution still frames, to provide the end product. Finally a README document will be generated to explain file organization, camera setups, and workflow.\n\n\nThe current folder size is significantly reduced compared to the original, but the parent project directory warrants further investigation for potential temporary files. Examining the `drift_frames` directory within the original project reveals a significant amount of data that can likely be removed. The audio file size is the dominant component of the `assets` folder, which should be assessed in the overall file size reduction strategy.\n\n\nThe user's instruction requires creating a slimmed project folder separate from the original. This will reside in a new `session` directory, containing the final Blender file, textures, and results.  A new folder structure will be created in the `session` directory.  The original project is safe.\n\nTemporary cache files like `__pycache__` and unnecessary render frames are targeted for removal in the original directory, to reduce its size.\n\n\nThe necessary directory structure will be set up within the new session directory, including folders for renders, assets, and textures. Textures and essential assets are copied into their designated new locations. I will ensure all texture paths in the Blender file are relative to ensure portability and then verify by headless render.\n\nThe final video, still renders, and curated hero shots are copied into the session's renders directory. A README file will be generated that describes the project's setup, controls, and rendering process. I'll make sure the file is descriptive for the user.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"$slimDir = \"d:\\项目session\\blender-supercar-slim\"\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory -Path \"$slimDir\\textures\" -Force | Out-Null\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory -Path \"$slimDir\\renders\\stills\" -Force | Out-Null\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory -Path \"$slimDir\\renders\\hero_renders\" -Force | Out-Null\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory -Path \"$slimDir\\assets\" -Force | Out-Null\n\"Created slim directories successfully\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolSummary":"Create slim directory structure"}}]}
